use /lib
from ./MainWindow import MainWindowScope
from /scope/container-environment import ContainerEnvironmentScope
from /scope/debug-log import DebugLogScope
from /scope/edge-releases import EdgeReleasesScope
from /scope/project-data import ProjectDataScope
from /scope/self-update import SelfUpdateScope
from /scope/user-preferences import UserPreferencesScope
from /ui/theme import ThemeScope

export scope ApplicationScope:
    concept: {ApplicationConcept}

concept ApplicationConcept:
    pitch:
        A REPL and sort of notebook for [Piton](https://piton-lang.org/spec.md)
        that allows you write prompts that references the spec and also allows
        you to build up a history of prompts saved as files.

    shape: {ApplicationShape}

shape ApplicationShape:
    description: desktop application

    stack:
        language: rust
        gui: gpui
        components: [gpui-kit](https://gpui-kit.com/)

    platforms:
        - windows
        - mac
        - linux

    childProcesses:
        On Windows, no program the application runs opens a window of its
        own. A release build on Windows is a GUI application with no console,
        so Windows gives every console program it starts a window of its own
        unless told not to. So every child process the application starts on
        the host, git, piton, for its builds, slicing, its language server,
        and the fluency, podman, the harness and its one-off runs, a run
        target's shell, and the commands that stop a process, is started
        without a console window, so nothing flashes on screen while the
        application works, in a release build as in a development one.

        What these programs print is still read as ever, and the login
        view's command, run under a pseudo-terminal, still works as the
        @{ContainerEnvironmentScope} says. On macOS and Linux, nothing
        changes in how a program is started.

    findingPrograms:
        Every program the application runs on the host, git, piton, podman,
        and each harness, Claude Code's `claude`, Codex's `codex`, and
        OpenCode's `opencode`, is found where the user's own terminal would
        find it, however Suspense was started. An application started from
        the desktop, the dock, Finder, or the Start menu isn't given the
        PATH the user's shell sets up, so the application never takes its
        own PATH alone.

        On macOS and Linux, it reads the PATH the user's login shell sets up,
        running the shell named by SHELL as a login, interactive shell once
        at launch and printing its PATH, with a timeout of 5 seconds, and
        searches that, then its own, then the places programs installed
        for the user are put, where they exist: `~/.local/bin`,
        `~/.cargo/bin`, `~/.npm-global/bin`, npm's global prefix's `bin` as
        `npm prefix -g` gives it, `~/.bun/bin`, `~/.volta/bin`, the
        active Node version's `bin` of nvm, fnm, or asdf, `/opt/homebrew/bin`,
        and `/usr/local/bin`. On Windows, it searches the PATH of the user and
        of the machine as the registry holds them now, not only as they were
        when Suspense started, then npm's global folder, `%APPDATA%\npm`, and
        each name is tried with each extension PATHEXT lists, so a program
        installed by npm as `codex.cmd`, or by a package manager as a `.bat`
        or `.ps1` shim, is found. A `.cmd` or `.bat` is run through
        `cmd.exe /d /s /c`, and a `.ps1` through PowerShell with no profile,
        with its arguments quoted for them, never passed to the system as
        though it were an executable, and still without a console window.

        A program counts as installed once it is found this way and runs:
        its `--version` exits with success. Where it is found but won't run,
        the Welcome check and a failed run say so, with what it printed,
        never that it isn't installed. Where it isn't found, they say it
        couldn't be found and name every folder that was searched, so the
        user can see why. The path each program was found at is shown in the
        Welcome check's row, muted, and what is found is looked for again
        each time the Welcome checks or Check again run, so a program
        installed while Suspense runs is found without restarting it.


    debugLog: @{DebugLogScope}

    fileOwnership:
        Every file and folder the application creates belongs to the user
        running it, as one the user made in Explorer or a terminal would, on
        every platform, so the user never has to take ownership of anything,
        or change its permissions, to edit, move, or delete it, or for git,
        piton, or the harness to use it. That goes for a new project's folder
        and everything written into it, the project's data under .suspense,
        the files a task or the editor writes, and the application's own
        files, as its preferences.

        On Windows, the application never asks to run as administrator: its
        build declares that it runs with the rights of whoever starts it, as
        invoked, and never requires elevation, nor does its installer start
        it elevated once it is installed, nor its self-update when it starts
        the new version. Should it be run elevated all the same, as from an
        administrator's terminal, what it creates is still the user's: each
        file and folder is made owned by the user who is signed in, never by
        the Administrators group, and takes the permissions it inherits from
        the folder it is in, never permissions of its own. The same holds for
        what the programs it starts create, git's repository included, so git
        never refuses a project for being owned by someone else.
        Nothing the application creates is ever left owned by Administrators,
        SYSTEM, or another account.

    name:
        The application is called Suspense.

    projectFiles: @{ProjectDataScope}

    theme: @{ThemeScope}

    mainWindow: @{MainWindowScope}
    userPreferences: @{UserPreferencesScope}
    edgeReleases: @{EdgeReleasesScope}
    selfUpdate: @{SelfUpdateScope}
